1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,283
A paddlefish is feeding in a river,

2
00:00:02,283 --> 00:00:05,303
in a place that will one day be called
North Dakota.

3
00:00:05,623 --> 00:00:09,386
It swims with its mouth wide open, sieving
the water for food.

4
00:00:09,707 --> 00:00:12,065
This morning, the water is full of glass.

5
00:00:12,965 --> 00:00:17,279
Sixty-six million years later, scientists
dug this fish out of the rock,

6
00:00:17,279 --> 00:00:19,736
with the glass still caught in its gills.

7
00:00:20,056 --> 00:00:25,357
That glass was melted out of the ground in
Mexico, three thousand kilometres away,

8
00:00:25,357 --> 00:00:28,719
by the asteroid that ended the age of the
dinosaurs.

9
00:00:29,039 --> 00:00:30,833
So how did it get here so fast?

10
00:00:31,733 --> 00:00:35,086
We followed the physics to this one
riverbank, minute by minute.

11
00:00:35,406 --> 00:00:40,291
And here, at this distance, the end of the
world arrives in a very strange order.

12
00:00:41,491 --> 00:00:42,771
It's probably spring.

13
00:00:42,771 --> 00:00:47,584
Growth lines in the bones of these fish
show they died in the season of plenty.

14
00:00:47,904 --> 00:00:52,252
The river winds through a warm forest of
flowering trees and conifers.

15
00:00:52,252 --> 00:00:54,923
Sturgeon and paddlefish cruise the
channel.

16
00:00:55,243 --> 00:00:56,573
A paddlefish's long,

17
00:00:56,573 --> 00:01:01,961
flat snout is covered in sensors that feel
the faint electric fields of its prey.

18
00:01:01,961 --> 00:01:06,817
Nothing in its world has ever needed it to
sense what is about to happen.

19
00:01:07,137 --> 00:01:10,573
Then, far to the south, beyond the curve
of the planet,

20
00:01:10,573 --> 00:01:15,884
a rock the size of a mountain slams into a
shallow sea at many kilometres per second.

21
00:01:16,684 --> 00:01:18,677
And at the river, nothing happens.

22
00:01:18,997 --> 00:01:22,615
The light of the blast gets here in a
hundredth of a second.

23
00:01:22,615 --> 00:01:24,785
But the planet itself is in the way.

24
00:01:25,105 --> 00:01:29,829
Three thousand kilometres of curved Earth
stand between this river and the crater.

25
00:01:30,929 --> 00:01:34,555
To be seen from here, anything rising from
the crater has to

26
00:01:34,555 --> 00:01:38,060
climb more than eight hundred kilometres,
above the curve.

27
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:40,599
The fireball does it in a minute or two.

28
00:01:41,399 --> 00:01:43,919
A glow climbs over the southern horizon,

29
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:46,943
silently, like a second dawn in the wrong
place.

30
00:01:47,263 --> 00:01:51,706
The fish notice nothing. Neither does
anything else. There is no sound at all.

31
00:01:52,906 --> 00:01:57,528
At the crater, the rock itself is flowing
like liquid. The floor rebounds

32
00:01:57,528 --> 00:02:02,213
into a peak taller than Everest, then
collapses again, all within minutes.

33
00:02:02,533 --> 00:02:07,038
A wave more than a kilometre high is
spreading out across the Gulf of Mexico.

34
00:02:07,358 --> 00:02:09,569
None of that can reach the river yet.

35
00:02:09,569 --> 00:02:12,796
But something else is already on its way,
underground.

36
00:02:13,696 --> 00:02:16,958
The first thing to physically reach the
river comes through the rock.

37
00:02:17,278 --> 00:02:21,660
The impact has rung the whole planet like
a bell. Its waves dive deep

38
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:25,978
into the mantle, where rock carries them
fastest, and curve back up.

39
00:02:26,778 --> 00:02:29,367
At five minutes and forty-seven seconds,

40
00:02:29,367 --> 00:02:32,473
the first wave arrives. A sharp jolt from
below.

41
00:02:32,793 --> 00:02:37,656
At ten and a half minutes, the second. The
ground starts to throw itself sideways.

42
00:02:37,976 --> 00:02:42,382
And at thirteen minutes, the slowest
waves, rolling along the surface itself.

43
00:02:43,182 --> 00:02:45,853
This is an earthquake bigger than any ever
measured.

44
00:02:46,173 --> 00:02:49,831
In twenty eleven, a far smaller one in
Japan set water

45
00:02:49,831 --> 00:02:53,894
sloshing in Norwegian fjords eight
thousand kilometres away.

46
00:02:54,214 --> 00:02:57,982
Here, the whole river begins to rock back
and forth in its bed,

47
00:02:57,982 --> 00:02:59,417
like water in a bathtub.

48
00:03:00,417 --> 00:03:03,776
Meanwhile, the crater has thrown molten
rock into space.

49
00:03:04,096 --> 00:03:07,687
Droplets of it, launched at five
kilometres per second,

50
00:03:07,687 --> 00:03:12,388
fly hundreds of kilometres above the
planet, on arcs like thrown stones.

51
00:03:12,708 --> 00:03:15,167
The ones aimed at this river come back
down

52
00:03:15,167 --> 00:03:17,855
twelve to twenty-five minutes after the
impact.

53
00:03:18,655 --> 00:03:22,334
They hit the top of the air at about five
kilometres per second.

54
00:03:22,654 --> 00:03:25,800
Each one becomes a shooting star. Then
thousands.

55
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:29,910
Then too many to count, across the whole
sky, in broad daylight.

56
00:03:30,810 --> 00:03:35,365
All that friction heats the air. For about
half an hour, the sky itself

57
00:03:35,365 --> 00:03:40,113
pours down more heat than the midday Sun.
At its peak, several times more.

58
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:43,303
How hot exactly is still argued over.
Enough,

59
00:03:43,303 --> 00:03:46,364
at least, to scorch anything caught in the
open.

60
00:03:47,464 --> 00:03:50,461
Higher up, a second, finer fallout is on
its way,

61
00:03:50,461 --> 00:03:54,560
from the hottest part of the fireball,
thrown up almost vertically.

62
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:58,726
It starts arriving over the river about
thirty-eight minutes in.

63
00:03:58,726 --> 00:04:00,408
It carries grains of quartz,

64
00:04:00,408 --> 00:04:04,435
crushed and scarred by pressures no
volcano or earthquake can make.

65
00:04:04,755 --> 00:04:07,456
That scarring is the fingerprint of an
impact.

66
00:04:07,456 --> 00:04:09,982
And it will be found here, in the same
mud.

67
00:04:10,982 --> 00:04:15,099
Each shooting star burns out within a
minute or two, high above the ground.

68
00:04:15,419 --> 00:04:19,216
But the droplets don't. They've cooled
into tiny beads of glass,

69
00:04:19,216 --> 00:04:20,700
and now they simply fall.

70
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:23,547
A bead the size of a grain of sand takes
most

71
00:04:23,547 --> 00:04:25,850
of an hour to drift down through the air.

72
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:31,472
So the first glass reaches the river about
fifty-five minutes after the impact.

73
00:04:31,472 --> 00:04:34,768
Smaller beads keep falling for the next
hour and more.

74
00:04:35,088 --> 00:04:39,711
They tick on the water. They sink, about
fifteen centimetres every second.

75
00:04:40,031 --> 00:04:42,757
And the paddlefish, still feeding, mouths
open,

76
00:04:42,757 --> 00:04:45,948
sieve them out of the water along with
everything else.

77
00:04:46,948 --> 00:04:49,961
The season matters. Here in the north,
it's spring,

78
00:04:49,961 --> 00:04:53,862
and animals are breeding and raising their
young, out in the open.

79
00:04:54,182 --> 00:04:58,767
On the other side of the equator, it's
autumn. One idea is that life there,

80
00:04:58,767 --> 00:05:03,230
slowing down for winter, was a little
better prepared for what came next.

81
00:05:04,230 --> 00:05:07,708
Somewhere in those first two hours, the
river turns around.

82
00:05:08,028 --> 00:05:12,322
The rock can't tell us the exact minute.
But it can tell us what came:

83
00:05:12,322 --> 00:05:17,474
a surge at least ten metres high, rushing
the wrong way, inland against the current.

84
00:05:18,274 --> 00:05:22,127
It tears fish out of the channel and
throws them onto the bank.

85
00:05:22,127 --> 00:05:24,756
Tree trunks, roots and mud tumble
together.

86
00:05:25,076 --> 00:05:29,114
Then the water drops back, and comes
again. At least two great pulses.

87
00:05:29,434 --> 00:05:33,516
And all the while, glass keeps raining
into the mud, layer after layer.

88
00:05:34,416 --> 00:05:37,152
Not everyone agrees on what drove that
water.

89
00:05:37,472 --> 00:05:41,552
The team that found the site argues the
quake set the river sloshing.

90
00:05:41,552 --> 00:05:46,165
A tsunami from the Gulf would have needed
at least eighteen hours to get here.

91
00:05:47,265 --> 00:05:50,366
By now, everything in the river is dead,
and buried.

92
00:05:50,686 --> 00:05:56,023
Light came first. Then the ground. Then
fire in the sky. Then glass, then water.

93
00:05:56,343 --> 00:05:58,622
There's one thing left that hasn't
arrived.

94
00:06:00,622 --> 00:06:05,342
Two hours and thirty-two minutes after the
impact, the river finally hears it.

95
00:06:08,542 --> 00:06:12,269
Sound is the slowest messenger. It crossed
three thousand

96
00:06:12,269 --> 00:06:16,258
kilometres of air at about a third of a
kilometre per second.

97
00:06:16,578 --> 00:06:19,570
And it got here to find a river already
filled in,

98
00:06:19,570 --> 00:06:22,023
under more than a metre of mud and glass.

99
00:06:23,023 --> 00:06:28,465
In the hours after, fine dust settles over
everything, a thin, peach-coloured layer.

100
00:06:28,785 --> 00:06:32,473
Across the planet, soot and dust spread
through the sky.

101
00:06:32,473 --> 00:06:36,293
Days turn dark. It will be months before
sunlight returns.

102
00:06:36,613 --> 00:06:40,635
About three quarters of all species alive
this morning will not make it.

103
00:06:41,835 --> 00:06:46,812
Sixty-six million years later, the river
is a dry hillside in North Dakota.

104
00:06:47,132 --> 00:06:50,310
And that thin, peach-coloured layer is
still there,

105
00:06:50,310 --> 00:06:55,170
on top of a jumble of fish, roots and mud
that settled in less than two hours.

106
00:06:56,070 --> 00:07:00,706
X-rays of the sturgeon and paddlefish show
the glass beads packed into their gills.

107
00:07:00,706 --> 00:07:02,438
More than half of them have it.

108
00:07:02,758 --> 00:07:05,730
The fish breathed it in before the mud
buried them.

109
00:07:05,730 --> 00:07:08,703
Which means they were alive when the glass
arrived.

110
00:07:09,023 --> 00:07:11,538
Many lie stacked on top of each other,
whole,

111
00:07:11,538 --> 00:07:14,891
lined up the way the water was flowing
when it dropped them.

112
00:07:15,791 --> 00:07:17,344
And on the old tree trunks,

113
00:07:17,344 --> 00:07:20,968
drops of resin caught the falling glass
and sealed it in amber.

114
00:07:21,288 --> 00:07:25,883
Untouched by water for sixty-six million
years, those beads are still glass.

115
00:07:26,203 --> 00:07:30,187
Their chemistry matches the black glass
found at the crater itself.

116
00:07:31,187 --> 00:07:36,197
Some scientists still want more proof that
this site captured the very first hours.

117
00:07:36,517 --> 00:07:41,178
But if it did, it's the closest thing we
have to a recording of that morning:

118
00:07:41,178 --> 00:07:44,144
a clock written in mud, with glass for its
ticks.

119
00:07:45,244 --> 00:07:49,170
One asteroid, one riverbank, one strange
order of arrivals.

120
00:07:49,170 --> 00:07:52,697
The sound came last, and nothing was left
to hear it.

121
00:07:53,897 --> 00:07:57,333
Next time, an asteroid that's coming in
our own lifetimes.

122
00:07:57,653 --> 00:08:02,568
On April thirteenth, twenty twenty-nine,
Apophis will pass closer to Earth than

123
00:08:02,568 --> 00:08:07,297
some of our satellites. We'll simulate the
flyby, and the impact we avoided.

124
00:08:08,297 --> 00:08:12,905
Apophis is already on its way. Subscribe,
and be here when it passes.

